  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported survey data to measure anxiety levels, and the observed mitigating effects of car ownership do not extend to the demographic groups reporting the highest baseline levels of ageing anxiety.",
      "what": "Using double/debiased machine learning on a nationally representative survey of 2,068 respondents in China, this study measures the relationship between household car ownership and ageing anxiety. The analysis reveals that owning a car significantly reduces anxiety related to ageing, though this protective effect is concentrated among men, urban dwellers, and younger adults, while showing no significant impact for women, rural residents, peasants, and middle-aged individuals.",
      "why": "The study establishes a direct link between personal automobility and psychological resilience to ageing, demonstrating that transport access serves as a psychosocial buffer rather than just a utility for physical transit."
    }
  },
